</style></head><body>
  <div class="cover">
    <img src="https://luna-assets-tsw.s3.ap-southeast-2.amazonaws.com/evelyn/02-cover-twelve.jpg" alt="">
    <p class="covertype">Laid for <b>${esc(j.first_name)}</b>
       &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; ${DATE(j.paid_at)}</p>
  </div>
  <div class="inner">
    <div class="intake">
      <p class="k">Your twelve, as they were laid</p>
      <p class="lede">A house is a room of a life. The wheel has twelve of them, and one card was
      laid in each, bringing different parts of life into the same reading.</p>
      <p class="lede">The rooms come in <b>six pairs</b>, each one facing the room across the
      wheel from it: what you own faces what reaches you through other people, home faces your
      standing, romance faces your circle. Where a room below sends you across to another, that
      pairing is why.</p>
      ${hers.map((p) => `<div class="rm">
        <p class="line"><b>${esc(p.house)}</b>&nbsp; ${esc(p.house_name)}
          <span class="ax">&#8596; ${esc(AXPAIR[p.house])}</span>
          <span class="c">${esc(p.card_name)}${p.reversed ? ' &middot; reversed' : ''}</span></p>
        <p class="gl">${esc(BLURB[String(p.house)] || '')}</p></div>`).join('')}
      <p class="lede foot">The pair marker &#8596; points to the room across the wheel. Nothing
      to learn — it is there so a line that sends you to another room makes sense when it does.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="process-note">About this reading: the cards are selected for your order. The interpretation is written with machine assistance in Evelyn's voice.</p>
    ${body}
    <div class="gift"><h2>And now the second part — this one is yours to keep</h2><p>When you have finished with the twelve, there is a small practice you can take from them. It asks for one plain observation each night and no promise that a pattern must appear.</p><p>Its purpose is to keep what happened separate from the story you first told yourself about it.</p><h2>What it is</h2><p>Twenty-eight nights. Four turns of seven. One line a night.</p><p>Each night before you sleep, you write down <strong>one thing you noticed that day that was different from the day before.</strong></p><p>Not what you hoped would happen and not what the reading told you to expect. Record the observable thing: a changed plan, an unexpected message, an offer, a refusal, a moment of relief, a silence where you expected an answer.</p><p>Ordinary handwriting, ordinary paper, no ceremony. If nothing was different, write <em>nothing was different</em>. That line counts. The page is a memory aid, not evidence that the reading must be true.</p><h2>The four turns</h2><p><strong>Nights 1 to 7 — the first turn.</strong> Just notice. Don&#x27;t interpret, don&#x27;t join anything up, and above all don&#x27;t act on it. This week you are only training the eye. Write the line and close the book.</p><p><strong>Nights 8 to 14 — the second turn.</strong> Keep the same one line. You may already have an explanation for what you are recording. Leave that explanation out of the entry. Facts are easier to inspect when the first theory is not written over them.</p><p><strong>Night 15 — the read-back. This is the night the practice pays.</strong> Do not write anything first. Sit down and read your fourteen lines in one sitting, in order, start to finish.</p><p>Mark what repeated, what contradicted your first explanation, and what remained ordinary. The page may contain a pattern or fourteen unrelated observations. Do not force one result into the other.</p><p>Then write your line for the fifteenth night.</p><p><strong>Nights 15 to 21 — the third turn.</strong> Continue the same way. Where you formed an explanation at the read-back, give equal attention to evidence that would disprove it. Do not monitor another person or collect a case against them.</p><p><strong>Nights 22 to 28 — the fourth turn.</strong> Keep writing without turning the ledger into an instruction. A repeated observation can be worth considering; it is not, by itself, a reason to confront, accuse, spend, leave, or make a formal decision.</p><p>On the twenty-eighth night, read the whole ledger through.</p><h2>If nothing happens</h2><p>Then nothing failed. The page says that these twenty-eight nights did not form a reliable pattern. Let that result stand.</p><p>You can stop there. If the practice itself helped you remember clearly, you may begin a fresh page and run it again. Start without carrying the old explanation forward and without asking the next page to confirm the first.</p><h2>And that is the gift</h2><p>Keep the book. Not for me — I don&#x27;t want to see it, and I&#x27;d rather you wrote freely than wrote something you&#x27;d be willing to show a reader.</p><p>Keep it because memory changes when a story becomes important. The page lets you return to what you actually noticed before deciding what it meant.</p><p>The ledger does not owe this reading a result. It gives your own observation somewhere steady to stand.</p></div>
    ${[0, 1].map((half) => `<div class="ledger-sheet"><h2>Your observation pages · nights ${half * 14 + 1}–${half * 14 + 14}</h2>
      <p>One observation each night. Record what happened before deciding what it means.</p>
      ${Array.from({length: 14}, (_, i) => `<div class="ledger-entry"><b>Night ${half * 14 + i + 1}</b><span></span></div>`).join('')}
      <h3>${half === 0 ? 'Read-back on night 15' : 'Read-back on night 28'}</h3>
      <p>What repeated? What contradicted your first explanation? What remained ordinary?</p>
      <div class="review-space"></div></div>`).join('')}
    <div class="colophon">
      <p>These twelve were laid for you and for nobody else. The writing is assisted &mdash; I
      use a machine to help me put a reading into words. The draw is real and it is yours.</p>
      <p class="sig">Evelyn</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</body></html>`;

